Subject: Keep a counted loop's bound register live across the loop

What this does
  Keep a counted loop's bound register live across the loop.

Why we need it
  The bound is a constant load at the loop header and the back edge re-executes it every iteration, so the value's live range ends at the header's compare and the allocator gives its register to a body value; the hoist then correctly refuses, and every ordinary loop kept the reload, the top test and no fused latch.

What it adds
  During the allocation liveness, grow the interval of a constant-push value that is defined and used in a block which is a back edge's target to the latch block's last instruction, so the allocator holds its register across the body; the post-allocation hoist and rotation then fire on the loops that already worked, and the B1 fusion follows.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
